2) Do not leave gaps in information just because you think “everyone” knows how to fill in
the gaps. That is, if you want to write point A and point C, do not leave out point B
because you believe the reader knows it. A responsible writer establishes a linear
movement of ideas whether or not some of the information is obvious. Thus, you
should keep in mind the following: The reader should never have to fill in the
blanks.
Example of a GAP
Another problem that is associated with data stealing is the increased attack surface.
[G] Earlier viruses, worms, and Trojan programs used to target only user-level
applications. [G] This is not true anymore. With increasing attacks on operating
systems using rootkits and other kernel-level malware, there are more avenues of
information stealing...
Another problem associated with data stealing is the increased attack surface. That is,
while earlier viruses, worms, and Trojan programs targeted only user-level applications,
they are now attacking operating systems with rootkits and other kernel-level malware.
Such methods create more opportunities for information theft.

3) Expand on difficult concepts in the following ways:

Ø Provide definitions, examples, details, analogies, or explanations.
Ø Paraphrase by beginning with That is or In other words

4) A responsible writer tells the reader what will come next at/near the end (by introducing
the new idea) AND sometimes at the beginning of sentences (e.g., “For one,”
Therefore,” For example,” “That is,).
5) In a paragraph, emphasize/introduce major points first, and then explain them in more
detail. Readers expect to get the main idea of a paragraph and the key points
immediately. They don’t want to have to guess or fill in the gaps, even if the idea is
obvious.
6) In sentences, emphasize/introduce major points near the end of the sentence. OLD
ideas always precede NEW ideas. However a NEW idea can come first as long as it is
subordinated (Because...., Although ....).

